// The C boundary: the types a C function is declared with, and the pointer C
// hands back.
//
// `extern("c")` was already enough to *call* C — the parser takes it, LIR
// carries the ABI and the backend emits a declaration. What was missing is the
// vocabulary to write the declaration in, which is this file.
//
// **The C types are aliases, not `distinct` types** (§11). `c.int` is a name for
// this target's `i32` and nothing more, so a language value goes straight into a
// C call and comes straight back out. A `distinct` type would put a written
// conversion on both ends of every call, and would be buying a distinction the
// language cannot use: what a C `long` *is* varies by target, and the target is
// already what decides it. §11.4's "coercions" are therefore nothing at all,
// which is the cheapest a coercion gets.
//
// **`ptr.<T>` is the exception, and it is its own type.** A `*T` is non-null and
// traced; a C pointer is neither. Erasing that difference is exactly how a null
// reaches code written on the promise that it cannot see one, so the two are
// kept apart and the conversion between them is a check.
//
// **A variadic C function is declared `#c_vararg`** (§9): the written
// parameters are C's *fixed* ones, and a call may pass a tail past them, each
// argument promoted the way C promotes one — anything narrower than an `int` to
// an `int`, a `float` to a `double`. `printf` is declarable, and so is `open`,
// whose `mode` is read only when the flags ask to create.
//
// What cannot be written is a `#c_vararg` function with a **body**: reading a
// tail is `va_list`, whose layout differs per target and which nothing here
// emits. Declaring is the whole of it, which is what a C library needs. `std`
// still formats things itself (`core/fmt`) rather than going out to `printf`.

{ C_LONG, C_ULONG, C_CHAR, C_VOID } :: import "target.nest"
{ transmute, size_of, make } :: import "mem.nest"

// ===< The scalar types >===
//
// Spelled with the sugar (`i32`) rather than with the family (`int.<32>`)
// deliberately: `int` below *is* `c.int` inside this file, so a use of the
// family here would resolve to the alias beside it. That is also why the three
// target-dependent ones are imported rather than written — see `C_LONG` in the
// generated `target.nest`.

// `char` is a third type, distinct in C from both `signed char` and `unsigned
// char`, and which one it matches is the ABI's choice.
@public char      :: C_CHAR
@public schar     :: i8
@public uchar     :: u8

@public short     :: i16
@public ushort    :: u16
@public int       :: i32
@public uint      :: u32

// LP64 and LLP64 disagree about this one and only this one.
@public long      :: C_LONG
@public ulong     :: C_ULONG

@public longlong  :: i64
@public ulonglong :: u64

// The pointer-sized set is `core`'s own: `usize` is already "as wide as a
// pointer on this target", which is the whole of what `size_t` says.
@public size_t    :: usize
@public ssize_t   :: isize
@public uintptr_t :: usize
@public intptr_t  :: isize

@public float     :: f32
@public double    :: f64

// ===< `void` and the opaque pointee >===

// C's `void` as a *return*: a function that returns nothing returns this. It
// is the language's own unit type, which is a real type with exactly one
// value — `f()` where `f` returns `c.void` is an ordinary expression — so an
// `extern("c")` declaration reads the way the C header does.
//
// It is imported rather than written, for the reason `char` above is: this file
// declares `void`, so `void :: void` here would name itself. See `C_VOID` in
// the generated `target.nest`.
@public void :: C_VOID

// C's `void` as a *pointee*: `void *` is `*c.anyopaque`, and `FILE`, `sqlite3`
// or any other handle a C library hands back is a `*c.anyopaque` too when the
// library does not publish the struct.
//
// It is an alias for the `opaque` primitive, which is the language's name for a
// type with no size and no values. Spelled apart from `void` above because C
// spells one thing two ways and the language does not: a return of nothing has
// a value and a pointee of nothing does not. The rule is `opaque`'s, unchanged:
// `*T` and `*c.anyopaque` convert only through an explicit `cast`, in both
// directions, and nothing reads through one.
//
// A binding that wants its own handle type — so that its `*Sqlite` does not
// interchange with every other C pointer — writes `Sqlite :: distinct opaque`
// rather than using this.
@public anyopaque :: opaque

// ===< The raw pointer >===

// A raw, nullable, untraced pointer to a `T` — what a C function takes and what
// one returns.
//
// It holds an address and nothing else, which is what makes it untraced: the
// collector sees an integer, so a `ptr` is never what keeps an object alive.
// That cuts both ways and is the point — a `ptr` into the GC heap is a way to
// lose an object out from under yourself, and a `ptr` is for memory C owns.
//
// One `usize` is also its whole representation, so it goes over the C ABI as a
// pointer does on every target here.
@public(all) ptr :: struct <T> { addr: usize }

// The null pointer. A function rather than a constant because `T` has to come
// from somewhere: `c.null.<u8>()`, or from the context it is passed into.
@public null :: func <T> () -> ptr.<T> { return ptr.<T> { addr: 0 } }

// A traced pointer as a raw one. Always sound — a `*T` is a valid address —
// and always a loss of information, which is why the way back is checked.
@public from_ptr :: func <T> (p: *T) -> ptr.<T> {
  return ptr.<T> { addr: transmute.<usize>(p) }
}

impl <T> ptr.<T> {
  // Whether this is the null pointer.
  @public is_null :: #inline func (self: Self) -> bool { return self.addr == 0 }

  // The address as a number, for a program that has to print one or compare two.
  @public addr_of :: #inline func (self: Self) -> usize { return self.addr }

  // `self + n` elements, in C's own arithmetic: the offset is scaled by the
  // element size. Nothing is checked, because nothing here knows how many
  // elements the C side allocated.
  @public offset :: #inline func (self: Self, n: usize) -> Self {
    return ptr.<T> { addr: self.addr + n * size_of.<T>() }
  }

  // The traced pointer, **checked**: a null here is the one thing a `*T`
  // promises its holder will never see, so it is a panic at the conversion
  // rather than a fault at the first use (§11.2).
  @public to_ptr :: func (self: Self) -> *T {
    if self.addr == 0 { panic("a null `c.ptr` reached `to_ptr`") }
    return transmute.<*T>(self.addr)
  }

  // The same, with write permission. Separate because permission over C's
  // memory is a claim about what C did with it, and one the program makes.
  @public to_mut :: func (self: Self) -> *mut T {
    if self.addr == 0 { panic("a null `c.ptr` reached `to_mut`") }
    return transmute.<*mut T>(self.addr)
  }

  // Read the `T` at this address.
  @public read :: #inline func (self: Self) -> T { return self.to_ptr().* }
}

// ===< C strings >===

// A pointer to NUL-terminated bytes.
//
// It is **`cstr`, not `str`**: a file that declared `str` would shadow the
// language's own inside itself, and `from_cstr` below returns one.
//
// The two do not convert implicitly in either direction, and cannot, because
// each is missing what the other has. A Nest `str` is a pointer and a length
// with no NUL anywhere; a `cstr` is a NUL and no length. Going one way costs an
// allocation and going the other costs a scan, so both are written.
@public cstr :: ptr.<uchar>

// What a `c"..."` literal becomes.
//
// The literal's bytes already end in a NUL — the lexer puts one there — so this
// is only the address of the first of them. It carries a `#lang` tag because
// the **compiler** is what reaches it: `c"..."` is desugared into a call to
// whatever claims the tag, exactly the way `f"..."` is desugared into calls to
// `Display.display`.
@public cstr_of :: #lang("cstr_of") #inline func (s: str) -> cstr {
  return ptr.<uchar> { addr: transmute.<usize>(&s.as_bytes()[0]) }
}

// The length of a C string, in bytes, not counting the NUL. The scan C's
// `strlen` is, written here so that reaching one does not need libc.
@public strlen :: func (s: cstr) -> usize {
  let mut n: usize := 0
  while s.offset(n).read() != 0 { n = n + 1 }
  return n
}

// A C string as a `str`, borrowing C's bytes rather than copying them: the
// result points into the same memory, so it lives exactly as long as C says
// that memory does.
@public from_cstr :: func (s: cstr) -> str {
  let n: usize := strlen(s)
  return cast.<str>(transmute.<[]u8>(Raw { ptr: s.to_ptr(), len: n }))
}

// A `str` as a C string: an allocation and a copy, because the NUL has to go
// somewhere and a `str` has nowhere to put one. The bytes are GC-managed, so
// the result is only valid while something still holds it.
@public to_cstr :: func (s: str) -> cstr {
  let b: []u8 := s.as_bytes()
  let out: []mut u8 := make.<[]u8>(b.len() + 1)
  let mut i: usize := 0
  while i < b.len() {
    out[i] = b[i]
    i = i + 1
  }
  out[b.len()] = 0
  return ptr.<uchar> { addr: transmute.<usize>(&out[0]) }
}

// The two words a slice is (`design/lir.md` §7b), so that one can be built out
// of a pointer and a length C supplied. Private: a program that wants this
// writes `from_cstr`, and a program that wants it for something else is doing
// something `core` should not be handing out.
Raw :: struct { ptr: *u8, len: usize }
